Dreams are such weird things...  especially if you've been
playing too much Nethack lately.

I was a priest in the year 1500AD, and the king of England
summoned me and told me a tale.

"My daughter has been abducted by the fell demon Demogorgon.
A knight brought me this news last night.  He was gravely
injured, and died before sunrise, but before he died, he
told me the following tale.

"'I was chaperoning your daughter on a horseback ride
across the meadows of the South Downs.  We stopped for a
picnic under a tree at noon.  After our meal, I grew
sleepy, and slept for an hour.  Only later was I able to
piece together what happened next.

"'Apparently, as I slept, your daughter rode on alone,
exploring the countryside.  She came across an old abandoned
manor house, and decided to explore.  On entering the house,
she encountered a white plastic box with a glass lid.  Inside
the box was a small grey kitten, mewing pitifully.  She did
not notice it's severely deformed front-left paw, or the
tuft of 8 purple tentacles on its belly.  Out of pity (or so
she believed), she unfastened the lid of the box and let the
kitten out.

"'The "kitten" immediately took on its true shape, that of
the foul demon Demogorgon.  Demogorgon had been imprisoned
in that magical Demon Containment Box for 500 years.  When
your daughter released him, he showed his gratitude by
putting your daughter in a state of suspended animation and
burying her in a coffin somewhere on the grounds of the
old manor house.  This story I got out of him when we fought.

"'Oh, yes, we did fight.  I woke from my slumber, and
followed the hoof prints of your daughter's horse to the old
manor house.  There I met the foul demon Demogorgon.  He was
as a spider, black and terrifying.  We fought.  I used all
my magic and all my weapons, and I was just barely able to
subdue him and return him to the box.  I never found your
daughter.  And one more thing, your majesty, you must...'

"But there the good knight died, Jesquel.  I want you and
your assistant to go rescue my daughter at once.  If you
are successful, I will pay each of you 10,000 zorkmids."

So I set out with my assistant, an old, paunchy, bald-headed
monk named Grumley.  We arrived at the old manor house
and began searching the place.  Grumley checked upstairs
while I searched down.  Everything went uneventfully until
the gnomish wizard arrived.

Suddenly a gnarled little old man dressed all in ugly grey
and brown rags was standing in front of me.  He pulled
a parchment scroll from his pocket, unrolled it with a
flourish, and began to read: "Akch!  Ggrazzet! Ahmqul!
Krachneh!  Gienve!  Kroichet!"  I cringed, because I
recognized the words of a Cursed Scroll of Create Monster.
The scroll incinerated in the wizard's hands and I was
immediately surrounded by three zruties, two gargoles,
two centaurs, and a minotaur, in addition to the gnomish
wizard himself.

I fought furiously with a blessed +3 broadsword in one
hand and a wand of striking in the other.  One by one
I cut down the monsters, until only the wizard was left.
He laughed in my face with a shrill cackle, "YOU'LL
NEVER FIND WHAT YOU SEEK, PRIEST!!!  I CURSE THEE!!!
HEE, HEE, HEE!!!"  I had had quite enough of him, so
I lopped his head off with my broadsword.

I was beginning to feel hungry, but the foul reek of
blood and ichor from all those dead monsters was
sickening, so I called out to my companion: "Grumley!
If you're finished searching upstairs, come on down!
I've finished searching down here.  I'm hungry!  Let's
go outside and eat lunch!"

Grumley came down, took one look at my disheveled
state (clothes torn and blood-smeared, skin scratched
and bleeding), and said "Good lord, boss, what happened
to you?  You look like you were mauled by a pack of
monsters or something!"

"I *WAS* mauled by a pack of monsters, you moron.  Lets
go outside to eat; it smells in here."

We went out onto the weed-grown courtyard in front of
the old manor house and rested by a ruined fountain
(dried-up) with a statue of a centaur at its center.
I ate an uncursed food ration, washing it down with
a diluted potion of banana juice.  Grumley ate lembas.

After lunch, I told Grumley, "Let's search the storage
shed on the other side of the courtyard next."

Suddenly it was evening instead of noon.  (Isn't it weird
how time jumps around in dreams?)  The sun had just
gone down and the courtyard was lit by the eerie purple
glow of twilight and the silvery light of the rising
full moon.  (I looked at the full moon and thought
to myself, "You feel lucky tonight, Jesquel!".)

We entered the old storage shed, but the light from
my tallow candle was not enough to show much more
than dim, vague shapes.  My companion Grumley mumbled
"Aw, boss, why are we blundering around in the dark?
Let's just turn on the light."  So I felt around near
the door, found the light switch, flicked it, and the
overhead fluorescent lights turned on.  (This, in the
year 1500AD!)

On the back wall of the shed were hanging an aklys,
a halberd, a rusty two-handed sword, and a dented
bronze plate mail with a squirrel's nest in it.  The
floor was covered with junk, including a broken
wooden wagon, a hubcap from a 57 Chevy, an old Philco
TV set, some wooden crates, and a black coffin.

I zapped a Wand of Probing, which worked rather
differently in my dream that it does in Nethack.  It
sent out blue streamers of light, which search a room,
and indicate the presence of any life forms.  One of
the blue light streamers spiraled around a spot on
the ground, and sank into it.  "I think we found the
king's daughter, Grumley.  Let's dig here."  I fetched
my blessed +3 pickaxe from my blessed bag of holding,
and Grumley wielded a dwarvish mattock.  We dug a
hole at the spot the wand had indicated, and sure
enough, there was a white coffin down there.  We dug
it up, pulled it out onto the dirt floor of the shed,
and opened it.  There was a beautiful blond-haired
maiden inside.

A few seconds after we opened the coffin, the maiden
awoke, climbed out of the coffin, and said, "Oh, wow,
you two must have been sent by my daddy to rescue me
from that awwwful spider!  Thanks, dudes!"  She
gave each of us a quick hug and a kiss on the cheek.
The she said, "I'm soooo hungry!  I think I'll just
hop on my moped and buzz on down to Taco Bell!
Toodeloo!"

She ran out into the courtyard, which was now in full
daylight, hopped on a Vespa, fired up its motor, and
drove away.  I muttered "stupid little rich girl...
doesn't even know the difference between a moped and
a Vespa."  (The fact that neither should have existed
in 1500AD didn't even occur to me.)  I further grumbled,
"I hope she doesn't get herself killed before reporting
to her daddy, or I'm out 10,000 zorkmids."

Suddenly it was night again.  The fluorescent lights
had gone away, and my candle had metamorphosed into
a brass lantern.  I told Grumley, "Now let's find
'that awwwwwful spider', as the young lady phrased
it."  I zapped my Wand of Probing one more time.
This time, the light streamer settled onto the black '
coffin.

With some trepidation, I opened the coffin.  There,
inside, was the white plastic box with the glass lid.
A Demon Containment Box.  And inside the box was
Demogorgon, and not in his kitten disguise, either.

In my dream, I screamed, and I'm pretty sure that in
real life I thrashed around in bed and cried out.
Demogorgon was truly terrifying.  He was a hideous black
apparition, vaguely spider shaped, with a wrinkly black
head, a mouth full of long sharp black teeth, and two
glistening black orb-like eyes that glowed from within
with malevolent green light.  His body was hard, scaly,
and black, and he had eight long, hairy, spindly black
spider-like legs.  He was in partially-suspended
animation, but he was still marginally aware, and I
could feel the hate and ill-will flowing from his
demonic black-and-green eyes as he gazed at me.

I threw a burlap sack over the box and told Grumley,
"Let's haul this demon home in our wagon."

Suddenly it was morning, and there were two horses
and a wagon in the courtyard.  We put the black coffin
in the wagon, and drove back to London.

I dropped Grumley off at his house, then I drove home.
I put Demogorgon on a shelf in my bedroom, but the
bag fell off the box, and I made the mistake of gazing
into the eyes of Demogorgon.  He bewitched me.
Suddenly he appeared to me as the world's cutest little
fluffy grey kitten.  I let him out of the box and took
him into my living room and showed him to my mother.
"Look, mom, I found a kitten!  Can I keep him?"

But my mom was dubious.  "A bit malformed, isn't he?
His front left paw is misshapen, and he has a tuft
of eight purple tentacles on his belly."

"Oh.", I said stupidly.  The subconscious part of my
mind suddenly realized that I had been duped by the
demon.  I meandered back to my bedroom, still petting
my kitten, who was purring heartwarmingly.
("Demogorgon pretends to be friendly!  Demogorgon
pretends to be friendly!")  Suddenly the subconscious
part of my mind flowed over into my consciousness,
and I jammed the kitten back into the Demon Containment
Box (which had shrunk to the size of a breadbox) and
slammed the door shut.  Even before the door was fully
shut, Demogorgon began to change back into his real
form.  The box quickly expanded to nearly the size of
a coffin to accommodate him.  He screamed with rage,
and his eyes were now blazing with red, orange, and
yellow light.  Hate, rage, ill-will, and magical curses
poured out of him and beat against me, but I was wearing
an amulet of Magic Resistance, which deflected his rage.

I put the box with Demogorgon in it back into the black
coffin.  I hauled the coffin to a cemetery, said my uncle
had died, bought a plot, and buried Demogorgon.  I put
a tombstone over the grave reading "I'm quite happy down
here!  Please don't dig me up!"


Then I woke up.


Cool dream, eh?


